summary:
Determines the density of monic integer polynomials of degree n with squarefree discriminant (Theorem 1.1): it is λ_n = ∏λ_n(p) > 0, which tends to ≈ 30.7% as n grows. It also shows that Z[x]/(f) is the maximal order with density ζ(2)^{−1} (Theorem 1.2). Both come with power-saving error terms X^{n(n+1)/2−1/5+ε}. Corollaries: ≫ X^{1/2+1/n} monogenic S_n-fields of degree n with discriminant below X, and the Ellenberg–Venkatesh conjecture that ≍ Y^{(n−1)(n+2)/2} fields have a generating integer of length below Y. The key is a tail estimate (Theorem 1.5) for polynomials whose discriminant is divisible by m² with m large. If p² strongly divides Δ, the quantitative Ekedahl sieve applies. If p² only weakly divides Δ, the polynomial is embedded (Theorems 2.3, 3.2) into symmetric matrices, in the SO_n-representation of Bhargava–Gross and Shankar–Wang, via explicit matrices B_m that split the two factors of p apart. There the new Q-invariant equals m, and the points of large |Q| are counted deep in the cusp by geometry of numbers. A squarefree sieve with κ-acceptable local conditions (Theorem 4.1) and bounds for reducible polynomials (Proposition 4.3) finish the proof. §5 converts polynomials into distinct fields by Minkowski quasi-reduction. The libraries have the discriminant, the index formula (so a squarefree discriminant gives a maximal order) and monogenicity. The atlas plans Hilbert irreducibility (IG.2), Siegel sets (AA.3) and the optimised Selberg sieve (SV.1, over Mathlib's Λ² upper bound). Everything else is routed as sources of ArithmeticStatistics ST.0–ST.3 and ST.5, with single items for GN.3, IG.2 and CA.3. Twelve source issues are recorded, none affecting the main theorems:
- the strong/weak criterion fails at p = 2;
- a wrong error term in the sieve (41);
- an index miscount in Proposition 4.3, which leaves the stated bound unproved for n = 4;
- a sign gap in Lemma 5.1, with an explicit counterexample;
- a missing case n = 2;
- several misprints, including the sign of w(Q) in (36).
The constructions B_m, the Q-invariant, Q² | Δ, the weight chains, λ_n(p), λ and C_3 were checked by computation.

routes.reason:
ST.3 owns counting theorems for fields by discriminant, with degree, Galois group and automorphism weights fixed. That covers:
- the density theorems for squarefree discriminants and maximal orders (Theorems 1.1, 1.2 and (4));
- the fact that a squarefree discriminant forces S_n;
- the counts of monogenic S_n-fields (Corollary 1.3) and of fields with short generating vectors (Corollary 1.4, with the Ellenberg–Venkatesh upper bound), with the quasi-reduction lemmas that make distinct polynomials give distinct fields (§5).
ST.5 records established asymptotics against models, which is where the conjectured asymptotic (42) and the constant C_3 of Remark 5.6 belong. None of these statements is in the atlas or the libraries.
